Yes — with one important correction: what you have is not “a framework that
automatically optimizes any phenomenon.” It’s closer to a general state-space
representation for systems that have measurable quantities, constraints, transitions,
and time.
That distinction actually makes the idea stronger.
Your Sudoku analogy works because Sudoku is not really about numbers. It is about
constraint propagation. A cell cannot take certain values because of its relationships with
its row, column, and region. Once enough constraints are known, possibilities collapse
until a valid state emerges.
